-- title:
--   Lemma 5, p. 17 — the dual solution generated by Algorithm 2 is feasible for the dual program (2)
-- statement:
--   Let $X_0=0,X_1,\dots,X_n$ be a run of Algorithm 2 on an instance satisfying the standing assumptions of §II. The dual update works as follows (p. 15). At the arrival of job $k$, take an optimal solution $(\alpha^{(k)},\beta^{(k)})$ of the dual of $\mathrm{RNF}'(r_k)$, the instance of the alive old jobs and job $k$ with their residual lengths. Set the dual variable of job $k$ to $\alpha^{(k)}_k$, and replace the tail of the β-variable from $r_k$ on by $\beta^{(k)}$. The resulting dual solution has
--   $$\alpha_k=\alpha^{(k)}_k,\qquad \beta_t=\beta^{(k)}_t\ \text{ for } r_k\le t<r_{k+1}\ (r_{n+1}=\infty),\qquad \beta_t=0\ \text{ for } t<r_1 .$$
--   It is feasible for the dual program (2) of the whole online instance.
--
--   Together with Lemma 6, this makes the dual-fitting argument of Theorem 3 work: the generated dual is feasible, and each arrival raises its objective by at least the algorithm's cost increment.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** Dual variables are per unit length ($a_k=\alpha_k/v_k$, as $v_k(r_k)=v_k$), and feasibility holds for almost every $t$. When several jobs share a release time, they arrive one after another in index order, and the β-segment of all but the last is empty.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--
--   - reading: the dual solution generated by Algorithm 2 (p. 15) → for each arrival k any optimal dual (A k, B k) of RNF′(r_k); a_k = A k k; β = B k on [r_k, next release), 0 before r_1; reason: the dual update of p. 15 ('its new optimal value α′n', 'the new optimal dual variable {β′t}t≥rn'), written out for a finite instance with tied release times; every optimal choice is covered
--   - encoding: jobs j = 1, 2, … arriving online with r_1 ≤ r_2 ≤ … (p. 18) → finitely many jobs Fin n with Monotone r; jobs with equal release times are processed one after another in index order; reason: the analysis is over a finite instance (proof of Lemma 5); ties are allowed by r_1 ≤ r_2 ≤ …
--   - encoding: dual (2): α_j/v_j ≤ β_t + g_j(t) for all t ≥ r_j, α_j, β_t ≥ 0 → per-unit variables a_j = α_j/v_j (residual length for GFCS(s)); β integrable on the horizon; β ≥ 0 and the constraints hold for almost every t; reason: β is a density on the time axis: changing it on a null set changes no dual objective; the pointwise form would make optimal duals ill-defined
-- source:
--   Etesami, An Optimal Control Framework for Online Job Scheduling with General Cost Functions, arXiv:1906.02644v3, p. 17, Lemma 5 (dual update: p. 15)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_GenCostSched_GFCS_Model

open MeasureTheory Set
open scoped ENNReal

namespace GenCostSched.GFCS

/-- Lemma 5 (p. 17): the dual solution generated by Algorithm 2 is feasible for the dual (2).
At the arrival of job `k` the dual update takes an optimal dual `(A k, B k)` of
`RNF′(r_k)` (alive old jobs and job `k`, residual lengths), sets `a_k := A k k` and replaces
the tail of `β` from `r_k` on by `B k`. -/
theorem lemma_5 {n : ℕ} (I : Instance n) (hI : I.Standing) (X : ℕ → Fin n → ℝ → ℝ)
    (hX : I.IsAlg2Run X) (A : Fin n → Fin n → ℝ) (B : Fin n → ℝ → ℝ)
    (hAB : ∀ k : Fin n, IsOffOptDual I.g (I.r k) (insert k (I.aliveOld (X k.val) k))
      (I.residual (X k.val) k) (A k) (B k)) :
    I.IsDualFeas (fun k => A k k) (I.concatDual B) := by sorry

end GenCostSched.GFCS
